    "zircon": {
      "label": "锆石",
      "path": "assets/gems/zircon.jpg",
      "query": "Zircon mineral",
      "title": "Iziko Mineral Zircon (cropped).jpg",
      "pageUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Iziko_Mineral_Zircon_(cropped).jpg",
      "sourceUrl": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/77/Iziko_Mineral_Zircon_%28cropped%29.jpg",
      "thumbnailUrl": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Iziko_Mineral_Zircon_%28cropped%29.jpg/960px-Iziko_Mineral_Zircon_%28cropped%29.jpg",
      "license": "CC BY 3.0",
      "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/3.0",
      "artist": "Nkansah Rexford",
      "credit": "Own work",
      "description": "A dark-coloured zircon crystal embedded in a light-coloured rock matrix, on display at the Iziko Museum in Cape Town. The dark colour may be due to natural radioactive decay (metamictisation) of the uranium and thorium content, where the radiation disrupts the crystal lattice to create colour centres. Inclusions and altered zones, including fluid inclusions, are often rich in elements such as uranium, thorium or rare earth metals, causing darkening, turbidity or opacity. Interaction with hydrothermal fluids can also alter the zircon, enriching it with elements such as Fe, Al and P, creating darker zones. Zircon often forms in layers (oscillatory zoning) with different chemical compositions, resulting in alternating light and dark bands. In South Africa, zircon crystals can be found in a white matrix such as white pegmatite (consisting of quartz and feldspar) or white calcite or carbonate rocks (associated with carbonatites). In the Barberton Greenstone Belt and surrounding areas, zircon is often found in white or light-coloured sedimentary quartzites, or in quartz-sericite schists. As in international deposits (and the Phalaborwa Complex), South African zircon can occur in white calcite or carbonatite rocks."
    },
